Title: What They Called Recovery
Credit: Animated feature screenplay — whole-film review draft v0.1
Author: Christopher Kokkinos
Draft date: 2026-09-28
Status: Combined scene-level review draft; SC001 accepted direction, SC002–SC057 pending review

# SEQ-01 — THE DRIVER AND THE ASSIGNMENT

= SC001 — Elias's apartment (accepted direction, DEC-0012)

INT. ELIAS'S FLAT - BEDROOM/KITCHEN - MORNING

Two PIGEONS stamp along the narrow window ledge. One pecks at the glass.

ELIAS, still in yesterday's clothes, opens his eyes. A clock beside a fake fireplace reads eight. The alarm switch is OFF.

He sits up. Pain catches beneath his left ribs. He steadies himself with his right arm; his shoulder objects too.

The bedside drawer is open a finger's width. Inside: a prescription blister strip of OXYCODONE. Two tablets are gone. Elias looks at it, then pushes the drawer shut.

Across the flat, the kitchen surfaces are clean. His clothes and unopened post are less orderly. A torch and a small set of tools by the door are precisely arranged.

He starts the shower. Hot water lasts seconds.

ELIAS
Excellent.

He dresses, makes coffee, and picks up a white envelope from the kitchen table. MAC is written on the front.

He opens it once more. The short notice inside reads: "I will complete work already accepted. I will not take further assignments after Friday. Please arrange final payment."

Elias folds the letter and puts it in the inside pocket of his jacket. He pauses with his hand there, then takes his keys.

At the window, the pigeons watch him.

ELIAS
Flat's yours.

He goes out. A pigeon pecks the glass again.

= SC002 — Mrs Doran (draft)

INT. ELIAS'S BUILDING - STAIRWELL/LOBBY - MORNING

The lift indicator holds at FIFTEEN. Elias presses the button. Nothing. He takes the stairs, coffee in hand.

On the third-floor landing, MRS DORAN struggles with a door that will not close. A wheeled shopping trolley waits beside her.

MRS DORAN
You're late.

ELIAS
You've been speaking to Mac.

MRS DORAN
I've been hearing you run down these stairs for nineteen years.

ELIAS
Twenty.

MRS DORAN
Then you've been late longer than I thought.

She pulls the door again. It jams halfway. Elias sets down his coffee, lifts the door by the handle and pushes it inward. The lower hinge settles. The lock meets the frame.

Mrs Doran tests it twice. Elias picks up his mug.

MRS DORAN
Work?

ELIAS
For the moment.

She notices his jacket, but lets the answer stand.

He takes her trolley. They descend together. At the lobby she retrieves the handle.

ELIAS
You still have my screwdriver.

MRS DORAN
You gave it to me.

ELIAS
I lent it.

MRS DORAN
That was in 2019.

ELIAS
So you admit possession.

She heads out. Elias waits until she reaches the pavement. He checks his watch. Later still. He goes on toward the mailboxes.

= SC003 — The clamp

EXT. ELIAS'S BUILDING/CAR PARK - MORNING

A FINAL NOTICE juts from Elias's overfull mailbox. He pushes it back and steps outside. At the next street his hatchback wears a yellow sticker and a wheel clamp.

ELIAS
You have got to be joking.

The permit is visible. The notice names the wrong street. Elias photographs the permit, bay, notice, street sign and clamp before he opens his boot.

He checks the disc and battery of a compact angle grinder. Sparks jump as he cuts only the clamp's replaceable arm. He waits for the disc to stop, places both pieces neatly by the sign and photographs them. The sticker goes into the passenger footwell. The grinder returns to its case with the battery removed.

He pulls out. His notice to Mac remains in his jacket. A neighbour watches from across the street, but Elias has already gone.

= SC004 — The call

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - CITY TRAFFIC - MORNING

The same red light returns after three cars pass. Coffee leaks onto Elias's fingers. He wipes them on his trousers. INSURANCE RENEWAL and RENT OVERDUE flash on his phone. A missed CLINIC call makes him pause longer. He turns the screen down.

Sirens. A police car crosses ahead, then an ambulance, then another police car. The ambulance travels between them. Elias brakes; his seat belt catches his ribs. He follows their arrangement with his eyes until the buildings hide them.

The X-Files ringtone. MR PRESIDENT on the screen. Elias answers.

MAC (V.O.)
Don't come to the office. I need you at the airport.

ELIAS
What happened to the assigned driver?

MAC (V.O.)
Unavailable. My niece is arriving alone. Zoe Harper. Thirteen.

ELIAS
Where are her parents?

MAC (V.O.)
With family. Take her to her grandmother.

Elias touches the envelope in his jacket.

ELIAS
That doesn't answer the question.

MAC (V.O.)
Find her. Keep her safe. Drive.

A horn sounds behind Elias. The light is green. He asks for the flight and a way to identify her. Mac promises a photo and ends the call. A flight number and a distant address arrive; no family contact or photo yet.

Elias studies the airport turn on the navigation, then indicates left. The unopened letter stays with him.

# SEQ-02 — THE GIRL WHO SETS TERMS

= SC005 — Processing

INT. AIRCRAFT/ARRIVALS CORRIDOR - DAY

ZOE, thirteen, holds her backpack close as an ESCORT in a navy coat leads her through the airport. Questions are answered from a folder. Zoe repeats a prepared explanation of a two-week visit. When the escort reaches toward her, Zoe pulls away.

Through the arrivals doors, the escort sees a man waiting with a phone. She turns down another corridor without introducing Zoe to him. Zoe catches the movement. No public handover happens.

Nearby, Elias has a parking ticket, a flight number and no reliable contact. A late image from Mac finally reaches him. He looks up. Zoe is already studying him.

= SC006 — Terms at arrivals

INT. AIRPORT ARRIVALS/PEDESTRIAN BRIDGE - DAY

Zoe reads Mac's message on Elias's phone but keeps distance.

ZOE
No touching. Tell me before you call anyone about me. And I keep my bag.

ELIAS
Yes. If we're about to be hit by a car, I will move you.

ZOE
Then tell me afterward.

He agrees. The escort is gone. Elias cannot give Zoe an independent reason why the driver changed. He doesn't pretend he can.

They walk beside reflective glass. Zoe uses it to check behind them. A frightened child nearby clutches a toy rabbit; Zoe helps return it without letting go of her bag. Elias waits without turning the moment into a question.

In the lift she watches his hands and the floor numbers. He lets her choose where to stand.

= SC007 — The car test

INT./EXT. AIRPORT CAR PARK - DAY

Zoe checks the boot, underside and rear-door lock. Elias notices her reaction when the child lock catches; he switches it off in front of her. He goes to pay the ticket.

Zoe opens the steering column and starts the hatchback. It rolls. Elias comes through the passenger door and stops it without laying a hand on her. Pain strikes his shoulder. The car settles; attendants are looking over.

ELIAS
The terminal is there. We can go back. Or you can come with me and watch the route. I won't put you in the car by force.

Zoe eyes the travel folder, then the open terminal. Neither feels simple. She chooses the back seat, an unlocked door, a visible route and no calls while he drives. Elias repeats the conditions so she can hear him accept them.

= SC008 — The public stop

INT. ELIAS'S CAR/SERVICE-STATION RESTAURANT - DAY

Zoe checks every junction against the navigation. A dark saloon appears behind them more than once, but they cannot identify it. Her stomach turns. Elias gives her sealed water and chooses a busy service station.

He stops before answering another private call. Zoe notices. In the restaurant she chooses a seat with the mirror and exit in view. Elias accepts it. He orders vegetarian food; when butter arrives by mistake, a WAITRESS replaces it. Zoe offers a first, guarded thanks.

ELIAS
You can take the water. I won't invoice you for it.

A hint of a smile, then Zoe sees a movement in the mirror. She says a decoy route was made before her flight. She thinks the real route was exposed. Elias listens, without treating her suspicion as proof about who told anyone.

= SC009 — Cain's men

INT. SERVICE-STATION RESTAURANT - DAY

Zoe names Cain. Elias's manner changes. He counts the entrance, the mirror and the people outside. A private caller says the girl must be sent out. Through the glass, two men wait near the entrance; the dark saloon sits beyond them.

ZOE
You know him.

ELIAS
I know enough to get us out.

He points to the toilets and the inside bolt. Three minutes. If he fails to return, she can go to the waitress and ask for airport police. Zoe repeats the plan in her own words. He leaves cash on the table and disappears through a service door.

= SC010 — Three minutes

INT./EXT. SERVICE STATION - DAY

Zoe tests the toilet bolt, the window and her watch. She waits. The lock's click holds her attention longer than it should. She looks at the unanswered message to Jackie.

ZOE
Only Jackie knew the real route.

She stops there. She has no way to know what happened to her friend.

After three minutes she unlocks the door. Elias is absent. She pays, hides her alarm and sees three men shift positions after an earpiece signal. Then Elias reappears, dusty and bloodied.

ELIAS
Walk on my left. Normally.

They pass families and a football rolling across their path. Zoe sees a senior man from Cain's house. He recognises Elias and hesitates. Elias keeps her door's inside handle usable. The car starts after a cough. Their pursuers scramble toward two vehicles, both with damaged tyres.

The hatchback reaches the slip road. Zoe sees Elias's injured hand on the wheel.

= SC011 — Seven days

INT. ELIAS'S CAR - MOTORWAY - DAY

Elias drives east, tests the empty road, then changes route again. Zoe audits each turn.

A message lights his phone: THE REAPER SHOULD HAVE STAYED DEAD. Elias turns it over. Zoe saw enough.

ZOE
Who was that for?

ELIAS
Someone who thinks they remember me.

He admits he disabled vehicles at the service station and that a man grabbed him. He will not teach her the method. Mac's story that Zoe is his niece no longer stands up to her questions; Elias will not claim he knows the truth of her destination.

ELIAS
I won't call Mac without telling you first. Unless waiting puts you in immediate danger.

ZOE
That's a condition, not a promise forever.

ELIAS
Agreed.

She looks at the backpack she will not hand over.

ZOE
It started Tuesday. Seven days ago.

# SEQ-03 — WHAT HAPPENED TUESDAY TO FRIDAY

= SC012 — The ordinary errand

INT./EXT. ZOE'S HOME/SCHOOL/STREET - TUESDAY - MEMORY

Zoe's voice starts in the moving car; the remembered day takes over. Helen is rushed. Brian leaves. At school Jackie and Zoe share a private joke. The house and friendship are ordinary enough that the short errand for milk needs no debate.

At the corner shop Zoe buys milk and chocolate. Four streets from home, an unmarked white van stops beside her. Two adults seize her. The milk breaks on the pavement.

Inside the van Zoe counts turns and pauses through fear. A garage door opens. The house beyond looks residential: curtains shut, a bicycle by the wall, numbered folders in a study. A woman offers unsolicited water and calls tomorrow "processing." Zoe is locked in a room with a high window and an empty wardrobe.

Back in the car, Elias asks what she could actually see. Zoe gives him the turns she remembers and says she did not know Cain's name then. He lets her continue.

= SC013 — The rules

INT. CONTROLLED HOUSE - WEDNESDAY/THURSDAY - MEMORY

Plain grey clothes replace Zoe's own. Six children wait in a room with too many chairs. An adult tells her not to ask questions or look the "buyers" in the eye. A younger girl falls silent under a glance. When Cain enters, the adults defer to him.

Sent to fill a jug, Zoe overhears talk of an older file. Through the study door: a numbered folder and a photograph of herself years younger. She keeps the number in her head.

Night gives way to another room, another light, another altered question. The television never stops. Zoe's sense of time loosens; she anchors herself to a stair creak, a floorboard and a cigarette smell.

On Thursday, the children are separated and inspected. An older girl murmurs that Zoe should not eat or drink before the showing. Zoe nods without announcing a plan.

Back in the moving car Elias offers to stop the account. Zoe chooses to keep going. At the time, she says, the old photograph and number were all she knew.

= SC014 — The showing and escape

INT./EXT. CONTROLLED HOUSE/BUS INTERCHANGE - FRIDAY - MEMORY

An adult gives Zoe a cup. She holds the liquid, spits it out at the sink and performs a drowsiness she cannot entirely separate from the effects she feels.

In uniform, she stands on a taped square before dark glass. Voices ask about documentation, medication, an original identifier and a transfer number. The answers conflict. An argument pulls the adults away.

Zoe waits. The study and safe are briefly accessible. She finds her childhood photograph, takes her folder and property bag, and sees a black drive attached to a laptop. She takes it without knowing what is on it.

As an injured man is moved through a service door, Zoe slips into the yard. She reaches two buses and home. The backpack stays on her lap through both journeys.

In the present car Zoe says she did not read the folder until just before the flight and still has not properly opened the drive. Elias does not call her escape theft. She made a choice with what she knew.

# SEQ-04 — HOME, DECOY AND WRONG DESTINATION

= SC015 — Home is changed

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - FRIDAY/SATURDAY - MEMORY

Helen embraces Zoe at the door. Brian asks who saw her enter, shuts the porch light and locks up. Zoe holds her backpack between them.

HELEN
We should call the police. And have someone look at her.

BRIAN
Mac first.

He calls. Mac orders no outside contact. Upstairs, Zoe overhears fragments through a vent: money, no proper adoption, Mac. Her own photographs look different to her. She hides the folder and drive before coming down.

Mac arrives and instructs them to send Zoe to his mother. He refuses police, hospital and Jackie. Zoe challenges the sudden grandmother and asks her carers how they looked for her. Their love is visible, but their answers fail. Elias hears this part in the car without providing an easy verdict.

Zoe mentions Jackie's story of a vanished watcher from an older violent job. A date lands badly with Elias. He will not explain yet.

= SC016 — Jackie and the decoy

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE/COACH STOP - SUNDAY NIGHT - MEMORY

Jackie makes Helen open the chain. She gives Zoe their deliberately wrong private challenge; Zoe answers. Jackie divides food into four portions before remembering that her three younger siblings are in different placements now.

JACKIE
After Mum died, Dad drank. I learned to count heads before I believed anyone's version of "safe."

Zoe knows the history. Jackie asks precise questions about the house and the people who held her.

On a science worksheet the girls draw two columns: what observers may see and what they may believe. They plant signs of a coach and ferry route, then Brian brings the actual boarding pass and Elias's photo. Jackie takes Zoe's coat and a spare bag. She will board the coach, leave early and call from a public supermarket.

ZOE
If you don't call, the real route is exposed. I don't travel.

Jackie agrees. When Helen and Brian try to send her away, Jackie says she can create a police record. They let her stay until after ten.

At 22:06 Jackie leaves. The promised call does not come. Zoe watches her phone until the screen goes dark. She has no evidence yet of why Jackie is silent.

= SC017 — The flight

INT. HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE/AIRPORT/AIRCRAFT - MONDAY - MEMORY

Zoe invokes the failed-route rule. Brian takes her spare phone; Helen admits the journey may be unsafe but helps prepare it. No police call is made.

Alone, Zoe reads the folder at last: original and assigned identity fields, placement entries, payments, Mac's legal name and Helen and Brian as receiving household. She sees no adoption order in the folder. She cannot yet know what an independent court record would show.

ZOE
I go because it may give me an opening. Not because he's right.

She takes the folder and drive with her. A driver hands her to an escort with prefilled travel answers. Staff process the paperwork. The escort leaves before the gate. Zoe keeps the drive close on the flight and studies Elias's photo as a person she may have to escape.

= SC018 — No grandmother

INT./EXT. ELIAS'S CAR/PUBLIC REST AREA - PRESENT DAY

Back in the present car Zoe names the placement fields and rejects Mac's grandmother story. Elias passes the assigned exit and cancels the navigation. He stops where there are people and two routes out.

With an old atlas he shows her a cottage under a former name. He cannot promise Mac does not know it. They weigh police, hospital, waiting publicly and travel alone. Zoe chooses the cottage provisionally, with conditions: her folder and drive stay with her; no Mac call; no moving her in sleep; leave if the site is exposed.

ELIAS
I treated you as a destination on a job.

ZOE
Don't do it again.

Mac messages that Elias does not understand what he is carrying. Elias destroys the SIM and isolates the handset. Zoe chooses the front seat. At a fuel stop she chooses food and the name she wants him to use: Zoe. They drive on with the car itself still vulnerable.

= SC019 — Jackie, unseen by Zoe

INT. WATCHED HOUSE - NIGHT

Jackie sits where she can see a locked door and a phone she cannot freely use. On a nearby screen, recent images of her siblings. A watcher enters; Jackie turns the screen away before it can be taken.

She is alive. She is under pressure. She says nothing about which route she gave, and Zoe is nowhere near this room.

# SEQ-05 — THE TRACKER AND THREE HUNTERS

= SC020 — The contract

INT. MAC'S OFFICE - NIGHT

Mac watches a marker on a map. Elias's car has passed the grandmother exit. The marker is not his phone; a transmitter was attached to the vehicle before pickup.

Cain calls. His lieutenant recognised Elias and feared the name he would not say. Mac resists Cain's claim to Zoe but conceals the warning as he contacts three contractors.

VOSS studies the thin driver profile. VALE asks who delegated custody, what authority supports the papers and why the backpack matters. REYES wants a search area. Mac describes a protected minor being recovered from an employee and offers live tracking.

VALE
These papers tell me what you want done. Who can make the decision for the child?

MAC
The people who signed them.

Vale does not accept that as an independent answer. The fee is high. The three take operational phones and depart with different doubts. Mac watches the signal fade toward the uplands. The map gives a radius, not a door.

= SC021 — First lesson

INT./EXT. OLD COTTAGE - TUESDAY/WEDNESDAY

Elias and Zoe inspect the cottage. She keeps her bag and a door she can secure. It belonged to an older part of Elias's life; he cannot certify its secrecy.

Under the hatchback he finds the transmitter. Its indicator still pulses. Zoe watches him reopen the tool case from the clamp morning.

ZOE
You told me the phone was off.

ELIAS
It is. I missed this.

He shows her terrain and normal traffic from a window, asks her to note changes rather than assume every car is hostile. She spots a vehicle checking the approach twice. Elias does not dismiss it or promote it to certainty. They decide to leave before the signal narrows them further.

= SC022 — Eastbound ghost

EXT. ROAD/TRAILER STOP/FARMHOUSE - DAY

They take a second vehicle and carry the transmitter away from the cottage. Zoe tests Elias's suggestion: if they send the signal east, what will the hunters infer? They secure it to a detached trailer that later travels in that direction.

At a less secluded farmhouse they check water, exits, a van, a ditch and a gate. Zoe draws three markers on a map for three possible pursuers. She writes what each would need to find them and what she will do if Elias is away.

ELIAS
You don't have to trust a place because I brought you to it.

ZOE
I wasn't going to.

= SC023 — Different methods

EXT./INT. COTTAGE/TRAILER/HUNTERS' VEHICLE - LATER

Reyes watches the cottage without entering. Vale traces an old ownership record, then inspects the place and photographs what is actually there. Voss follows the moving transmitter east and recovers it from the trailer.

At their regroup the positions do not add up to a current address. Vale notes evidence of a girl who watched and planned; Reyes calls it luck. Voss moves Zoe from passive passenger to active observer on their working map. None of them has located the farmhouse.

# SEQ-06 — OBSERVATION, TRAP AND SAM

= SC024 — Market observation

EXT. MARKET SQUARE/SHOPS - DAY

Elias and Zoe shop from a short list. He points out how a pursuit changes what surrounds its target. Zoe uses a shop reflection to identify Reyes at a flower stall. Elias alters course on her word.

They move through ramps and parking levels, testing whether ordinary traffic follows. Zoe sees Vale with a different bag but the same worn shoe. The pair leave without leading either hunter to the farmhouse.

Later, Voss and Vale acknowledge she spotted the relay. Reyes hears only that a child beat him. His impatience hardens.

= SC025 — A fallback and a bait room

EXT./INT. FARMHOUSE/LOCK-UP - DAY

Zoe practices reversing the farm van inside the yard. A map, cash, alarm and her backpack are set where she can find them. She tests the ditch route and a deadline if Elias does not return.

Elias prepares a rentable lock-up using traces from an old trainee connection. No belonging of Zoe's goes into the bait. He tells her he expects Reyes to come and that he means to trap him.

ZOE
Don't call this my plan because you told me about it.

ELIAS
I won't.

She repeats her own fallback, then asks whether there is another way. He cannot offer one he believes will stop Reyes. Elias leaves by motorcycle; Zoe begins her watch rather than merely waiting to be rescued.

= SC026 — Reyes enters

INT./EXT. LOCK-UP/FARMHOUSE - DAY

Vale finds the bridge to Elias's old identity and warns the team away. Voss orders Reyes to hold. Reyes sees signs of occupancy and enters alone.

Elias attacks from the adjoining partition. The struggle is close and costly. Reyes dies. Elias takes the operational phone, sees Voss and Vale's names, then stands over the body longer than he wants to. His hands shake as he prepares to leave.

Back at the farmhouse he tells Zoe exactly what happened and that he staged the room. She rejects his claim that Reyes made a fully free choice by entering. The killing belongs to Elias, not to her fallback.

ZOE
Who was the person you taught?

He cannot answer yet. Voss and Vale arrive at the lock-up after the police and see the consequences of their incomplete brief.

= SC027 — Sam

INT. FARMHOUSE - MORNING

Zoe asks again. Elias tells her about Sam, a young driver who became his trainee and a person he cared for. A job presented as observation concealed more armed people and a compromised extraction. Sam died in the ambush. Elias's reprisals made a reputation other men still whisper about.

His account comes in fragments of conversation and brief images, never an omniscient replay of every fact. He does not know exactly how much was concealed by whom.

ELIAS
I thought if I taught him enough, he'd come home.

ZOE
You didn't write the brief. You didn't fire at him. And I'm not Sam.

Elias looks at the observation notes she made without him. He accepts that protecting her cannot mean deciding what she may learn. He resumes teaching, this time asking which part she wants to practice.

# SEQ-07 — A MEETING THAT CANNOT TRANSFER ZOE

= SC028 — Contact

EXT./INT. LOCK-UP AREA/MAC'S OFFICE/FARMHOUSE - DAY

Voss and Vale withdraw from the police response to Reyes's death. Mac demands an update; they demand to know why he hid Elias's risk and what his authority over Zoe actually rests on. The contract supplies words, not an independent answer.

At the farmhouse Elias considers Reyes's phone. Voss and Vale keep a clean handset apart from their old channel. Zoe insists on attending any meeting.

ZOE
If I'm the reason you're meeting, I am in the room.

Elias initiates one bounded contact. Voss receives it. Each side assumes the other may be monitored.

= SC029 — Public terms

INT. CITY CAFÉ - DAY

Elias and Zoe sit with exits in view. Voss and Vale arrive separately. The public room constrains everyone, including their choices about force.

Voss shows Mac's recovery terms. Vale admits that the delegated paperwork survived casual checks but they did not independently confirm the supposed guardian's authority. They warn a replacement team may come. Elias wants names and routes; Zoe wants to know why Mac called her property.

Vale suggests taking Zoe aside while adults settle an exit. Zoe stays seated.

ZOE
People keep calling it a safe handover when the person being handed over can't say no.

Voss withdraws the proposal. The men are not yet allies. Then Zoe notices a watcher beyond the café's own coverage. Someone used a route the contractors thought isolated.

= SC030 — The delivery van

EXT. CAFÉ/KITCHEN LANE/CITY STREETS - DAY

Mac's additional watchers close on the café. Zoe identifies a delivery van and a route not included in either side's plan. Elias checks the driver's movement and the public sightline. He follows her lead.

Voss and Vale hold the watchers long enough for the pair to leave through the service lane. They do not take Zoe, her bag or her drive. On a different street, Zoe and Elias compare what the hunters disclosed with what they actually did. Their help is evidence of one choice, not a guarantee of future trust.

= SC031 — The index

INT. CASH HOTEL ROOM - NIGHT

Blank drives, an offline laptop and a table cleared of everything else. Zoe keeps the original. Elias makes working copies with her permission and separates them.

They find placement, monitor, recover and exception fields. Numbers connect her folder to routes and transactions, but no row explains itself. Zoe writes beside a code: AC019 — probably Mac. PROVE IT.

ELIAS
We could show someone all of it.

ZOE
All the children in it? Their routes and names? We need one thing someone outside this can check.

He steps back from the keyboard. Zoe chooses the next question.

# SEQ-08 — A NARROW QUESTION BEATS A LARGE ACCUSATION

= SC032 — Routes and exceptions

INT. HOTEL WORKROOM/VALE'S RECORDS TABLE - DAY

Zoe compares route codes with roads, crosses out a too-simple pattern and counts transitions rather than children. An ordinary path and a manual override can coexist in the same system. Her first search of her own root shows no direct BRG; a linked predecessor changes the picture without proving every earlier step false.

Across the city Vale traces an older case through paper routes. A child and destination recur while the claimed authority changes. He and Voss remember only the part of the job they performed. They do not know the outcome after delivery.

ZOE
A matching route isn't a matching reason. What did the original authority actually permit?

Neither table can answer yet. Current access also suggests a leak or insider; Elias and Zoe leave both possibilities open.

= SC033 — Recipients

INT. RENTED OFFICE - DAY

Zoe opens household suitability records, match scores, preferences and blank child links. Some preparation predates a child's arrival. A score cannot establish what a household knew.

Vale recalls a crying adult at an earlier handover. He once read it as an ordinary foster placement; now he cannot say she understood the papers any better than he did.

Zoe arranges four columns: houses, jobs, matches, leverage. She stops when a loving home is reduced to an attachment score. She leaves one private image unopened.

ZOE
If someone loved a child, that could make the beginning harder to question. It doesn't tell us what they knew.

The possibility points toward Helen and Brian. Zoe is not ready to open their entire file.

= SC034 — The limited query

INT. RENTED OFFICE/MAC'S OFFICE - DAY

The original drive stays with Zoe. Elias has one copy; a third is elsewhere. They set a documented ordinary relocation beside the case they want to test, choose a placement identifier rather than Zoe's name and record the expected normal response before querying.

A public channel says archive migration has made the record unavailable. Behind it, restricted review continues and a sponsor advisory moves toward AC019. The elapsed minutes matter. The public answer alone would prove little; the parallel path changes its meaning.

Mac sees a warning without seeing Zoe's whole test. Zoe stops when exhaustion blurs her judgment. She refuses to query thousands of child records to improve a guess.

= SC035 — Circular answers

INT./EXT. TRANSIENT WORKROOM/VERIFICATION ROUTES - DAY

Zoe defines the outside check: no child names, no location, no public accusation. A limited fragment reaches Voss and Vale by a short-lived intermediary. They verify only what they received.

A clerk cannot confirm a general record and points them to another number printed on the supplied papers. That number confirms the same version. Zoe draws arrows between the sources until they form a circle.

Cain notices that someone is comparing channels. He tells Mac to observe rather than strike. Their attention means later silence is no longer a neutral baseline.

ZOE
We need the original they copied. One answer outside this circle.

= SC036 — Two orders

INT. WORKROOM/SOLICITOR'S OFFICE - DAY

On the table: two versions of a family-court order. The case, hearing and judge details agree. The operative paragraphs do not. Zoe locates a source image, a text extraction and a seal map; the base order includes supervised contact and review. The contractor version changes authority and destination.

The supplied contact confirms the altered version. A solicitor's independent court copy does not. Names and child initials stay redacted in their working example.

ELIAS
Someone using that version could call a parent a threat.

ZOE
One order. One case. That's what we can prove so far.

They move lodging after the test. Cain knows the channel comparison threatens him but not where Zoe has gone.

# SEQ-09 — THE HOUSEHOLD AND THE INDEPENDENT CONTRADICTION

= SC037 — A profile is not a person

INT. NEW LODGING/VALE'S ROOM - DAY

Zoe opens one recipient profile. Earlier viewing, a prior child, recognition and preferences have been turned into match criteria. She can see how a family's ordinary longing might be used without knowing whether they understood the mechanism.

Vale remembers the sponsor's reassuring explanation at an old handover. It sounded protective, and he did not call anyone outside that chain.

Zoe closes a private image before it fills the screen.

ZOE
I'm not making another child into a score just because this file did.

She records the fields necessary for the inquiry, leaving the image alone. A household can love a child and still make a consequential choice about the beginning.

= SC038 — Reaction

INT./EXT. TEMPORARY WORKSPACE/MERIDIAN SITES - DAY

Three narrow prompts go out with expected response times noted in advance. Voss watches a contractor portal; Vale watches a physical route. One reply is routine. Another triggers restricted coordination. A Meridian participant moves between sites; Vale records the movement, not an invented conversation.

Zoe compares the response with the long quiet history of Helen and Brian's placement. She wonders whether a household stability check followed an earlier call. She writes QUESTION beside that hypothesis.

ELIAS
You're allowed to leave a question open.

ZOE
I know. I just don't like it.

= SC039 — Helen and Brian's file

INT. NEW LODGING/HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE - EVENING

P06-11803: an application, a break, Mac's later contact, a reopened file. Zoe separates these dates. The coded payments and monitoring entries include Brian and a joint account, but none is labelled a price for a child. She sees questions about attachment and outside contact.

At home Helen stops herself from calling the familiar number. Brian still wants certainty Mac says he can provide. Their fear for Zoe does not settle what they knew years ago.

Zoe finds indications she may have been shown to them before placement. Anger rises; she does not open every image.

ZOE
Mac found a door into it. That doesn't mean he owned the whole building.

Elias brings food and waits while she closes the file and writes down only what she can support.

= SC040 — The real organisation

INT. WORKROOM/WESTMERE OFFICE - DAY

Zoe samples Westmere cases: ordinary reunifications, lawful placements, uncertain records and a smaller suspicious subset. She labels columns NORMAL, SUSPECT and UNKNOWN.

Vale speaks with a retired contact about what Westmere can and cannot authorise. The contact gives limits, not a list of guilty people. A narrow shadow route borrowed forms and trust from lawful departments; most staff would see only their own part.

ZOE
If we call every worker part of it, we lose the people who can show what really happened.

They prepare a question about a particular supplemental order, with child identities protected. The whole system is not the allegation. The altered authority is.

= SC041 — North Vale answers

INT. NORTH VALE OFFICE/WORKROOM - DAY

PRIYA at North Vale receives the neutral inquiry for case H09-55216. The underlying case exists. The supposed supplemental authority cannot be found, and the power it claims does not match the real order. Priya opens an independent file and sends a bounded written answer.

Vale lays dispatch, placement and review dates beside it. One destination appears prepared before the purported authority existed. Zoe keeps the authenticated source with that timeline.

Mac and Cain hear that someone outside their channels has asked the question. An independent record now exists that neither controls.

ZOE
We can say this part. Base case real. Supplement missing. Claimed power disputed. The rest stays a question.

= SC042 — The yellow coat

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S WORKROOM - NIGHT

Voss opens an old job file. A girl in a yellow coat, frightened at a handover. Reyes gave her his own coat. They believed they were helping. Vale now places the receiving-house preparation beside the later claimed authority; the dates run the wrong way.

A temporary carer challenged another move and was sent to the verification number on the disputed papers. The answer came from the same supplied chain. No outside check was made by the team.

Voss writes a bounded account: what he did, what the documents told him, and what he did not verify. Zoe reads it without offering absolution. Vale remembers Reyes's kindness and his later choices together.

VOSS
We can say what we did. We can't speak for every family in the archive.

# SEQ-10 — THROUGH GLASS; JACKIE REACHES OUT

= SC043 — A record beyond the drive

INT. WESTMERE COMPLIANCE/NORTH VALE OFFICE - DAY

ELAINE FOSTER opens Westmere's copy of the disputed case. The migrated entry differs from older material; a protected placement account is attached where it should not be. She asks a retired colleague, MARTIN HALE, about the old protected desk. He has a notebook about workflow, no child records.

Elaine samples ordinary cases too. Then an urgent reconciliation and courier request collides with legal preservation. She records who sent it and when rather than assuming every colleague is hiding something.

Priya asks Voss and Vale for a supported contractor example. Their bounded written account can be checked outside Zoe's drive. The inquiry continues even if Zoe chooses not to expose the archive.

= SC044 — Cost of containment

INT. MAC'S OFFICE/HELEN AND BRIAN'S HOUSE/LAUNDRETTE - DAY

Mac and Cain disagree about moving records. Preservation, security costs and each fresh instruction leave present-day traces. Neither can erase an outside inquiry by ordering a courier.

Helen rejects Mac's assurance that Zoe is safe and ends their habitual call route. Her present refusal does not erase her earlier acceptance.

Zoe and Elias do laundry and argue lightly over food. Zoe still decides where her copies stay and what he may tell the contractors. Voss and Vale accept that their own statements can be used without taking her original. Ordinary choices continue inside the investigation.

= SC045 — The match

INT. TEMPORARY ROOM/HELEN'S HOME - EVENING

Zoe chooses to open the part of her match she avoided. Helen and Brian lost a daughter. Their lawful attempt to adopt failed. Mac then approached, and the file was reopened. The record shows scoring and acceptance, not proof they knew every falsified order.

Helen looks through photographs of the child she lost. She remembers seeing resemblance where there was a different girl. In Zoe's file, a high attachment score made a stable household useful to those watching it.

Zoe shuts the laptop and eats with Elias. She does not have to deny birthdays, bicycles or comfort to question the first decision. She does not have to answer Helen today.

= SC046 — Through glass

INT./EXT. OLD ASSESSMENT CENTRE/WORKROOM - DAY

Voss finds a booking for an observation room. Helen and Brian separately remember Mac bringing them after hours, the dark pane and the small girl beyond it. Their memories differ in detail but meet on the decision: Brian suspected the resemblance had been arranged; Helen knew there might still be family. They accepted anyway.

Zoe studies the plan. She goes to the building, stands outside and decides not to enter. She closes her notebook. No speech tells the audience how to judge her feeling.

= SC047 — Three messages

INT. VOSS AND VALE'S WORKROOM/ELIAS'S CAR - DAY

An image of three messages reaches Voss from an unknown number. Jackie says she was forced to give the first route, withheld the second, and her siblings are alive. The sender is likely Jackie; the number is not proof by itself.

Elias stops the car before showing Zoe. She reads each message. Her old conclusion about betrayal breaks, but she will not declare the phone safe or the sender authenticated because she wants it to be true.

ZOE
We had a question nobody else would answer right.

= SC048 — Jackie answers

INT. WATCHED HOUSE/TEMPORARY ROOM - DAY

Jackie has found a narrow chance to use a phone. Cain's people hold current evidence of her three younger siblings and can threaten them. The audience now sees more of the room first glimpsed in SC019.

Zoe sends their deliberately wrong challenge. Jackie returns the private answer. It confirms their shared history, not the safety of the device or house. Jackie tells Zoe the first route was coerced; she did not supply the second. Zoe can be hurt by the silence and understand the force behind it.

ZOE
We'll get you out.

JACKIE (TEXT)
Not if they can move the others first.

They exchange only limited questions through Voss. Jackie reports disagreement inside the legal chain and an impending move. Mac learns a phone was used but not what passed. They have a window, not a complete plan.

# SEQ-11 — THE SECOND DECOY OPENS A REAL PROCESS

= SC049 — Partial plans

INT./EXT. SEPARATE WORKSPACES - NIGHT

Jackie knows her move. Voss knows one signal route. Vale knows the paper contradiction. Zoe knows what she will disclose. Elias knows an escape fallback. No one receives a master plan.

A legacy channel keyed to AC019 carries a narrow message about P06-11803's disputed authority and the old presentation site. A vehicle hire adds a plausible movement. Cain takes the bait and heads toward the assessment centre.

Zoe checks one last time what Jackie has agreed to. Her friend rejects an improvised extraction that could endanger the siblings. The plan exposes a disputed transfer; it does not promise control over the independent response.

= SC050 — The safeguarding call

EXT./INT. SERVICE AREA/MAC'S OFFICE/LEGAL ADMINISTRATION - DAY

Jackie waits during a restricted move. Mac obtains a duty safeguarding contact from Margaret. He gives his own name, says a child is being moved under disputed authority, and gives Jackie's current location. The contact does not give Mac control of where she will go.

After the independent team is dispatched, the legal administrator refuses to continue the transfer on the contested order. SARAH and LEANNE arrive. Jackie checks their names and credentials before leaving with them, asks about her siblings and requests independent advice.

Elsewhere Cain reaches the bait site. His arrival and Jackie's safeguarding are separate events. Zoe receives an update marked UNCONFIRMED and has to wait rather than call it a rescue.

= SC051 — Inside and outside

INT. SAFEGUARDING OFFICE/ZOE'S TEMPORARY ROOM - DAY

Jackie tells CLAIRE what she wants recorded: protect her siblings, no unwanted participants, no premature contact with anyone claiming to speak for her. Claire writes it down.

Priya and other investigators look for a way to hear Zoe outside Mac's, the carers' and Elias's claims. Zoe checks a possible contact and speaks to RUTH. Ruth admits what she does not yet know and starts a separate record of Zoe's wishes. Zoe can say "I don't know" about where she will live.

Jackie may choose when to see Zoe. Zoe accepts that a process she cannot command can still protect her friend. Elias stays beside her without asking Ruth to treat his account as hers.

= SC052 — Owned conduct

INT. INTERVIEW ROOMS/SOLICITOR'S OFFICE - DAY

Voss and Vale separately sign bounded statements about the old job, their reliance on supplied confirmation and their failure to check outside it. They include the historic identifier, not unverified claims about all children.

Helen and Brian separately describe the through-glass viewing, their doubts and the choice they made. A household-stability confirmation links P06-11803 to the opening of Zoe's recovery status. It supports a specific connection without deciding everything they knew. Mac takes the sponsor-introduction agreement to counsel, aware disclosure will not grant immunity.

A legal participant refuses Cain's contested move; a surveillance request concerning the carers is declined without lawful instruction. Fragmented loyalties create records Cain cannot simply command away. Zoe is offered these accounts, but chooses when and whether to read them.

= SC053 — Irreversible

INT. RUTH'S OFFICE/POLICE EVIDENCE ROOM - DAY

Zoe rejects a proposal to "release everything." With Ruth and a police contact she identifies the altered order, North Vale's answer and the limited records needed to test them. She asks who can copy them, who can see the names and what cannot honestly be promised.

Police preserve logs, templates and routing; they obtain signed contractor and adult accounts. Mac cooperates under counsel. Cain's old channels no longer give him the same reach. None of this decides the historic cases at once.

Zoe crosses out a triumphant word in her notebook and writes IRREVERSIBLE. She closes it herself.

# SEQ-12 — DIRECTION

= SC054 — Custody

INT. SECURE EVIDENCE OFFICE - TUESDAY

Ruth holds a separate sealed envelope. Zoe carries the original drive in the backpack she has guarded since the airport. An evidence officer records her signature, bags and seals the drive with restricted access and an audit trail. Earlier working copies remain separately accounted for.

The backpack is nearly empty. Zoe keeps it anyway. The handover has an identifiable recipient and limits; nothing has been published wholesale.

= SC055 — Jackie and Zoe

INT. QUIET MEETING ROOM - DAY

Jackie enters only after both girls have agreed to meet. Zoe gives her room to sit or leave. They speak about the forced first route, the withheld second, and the time each believed the other had chosen silence. Jackie asks after the next steps for her three siblings; no one describes them as objects simply to be retrieved.

Their familiar, absurd challenge returns as a joke instead of a test. They laugh, then sit through a quiet moment without making it tidy.

= SC056 — A life still being decided

INT./EXT. VARIOUS - LATER THAT WEEK

Cain faces charges concerning Jackie's coercion and interference with safeguarding. Mac is charged later over placement records, conspiracy and finance while continuing to cooperate. Investigations into older cases proceed. These are charges and inquiries, not convictions or a solved archive.

Zoe reads Helen's letter when she chooses. Helen admits she knew enough to refuse at the beginning and accepted anyway. Memories of ordinary years with both carers remain real. Brian's separate letter waits; Zoe is not asked to forgive, return or permanently reject anyone on a timetable.

Elias attends to his pain and the dependence he fears repeating. In Ruth's independent record Zoe asks to be called Zoe and speaks about where she might live and whom she wants to see. No adult signs that wish on her behalf.

= SC057 — The ridge

EXT. PATH JUNCTION - SATURDAY

Zoe and Jackie have talked about the difference between leaving a room and being heard afterward. Now Elias and Zoe walk together. He says he may go away because distance could keep her safer.

ZOE
Is that what you want? Or did you decide what I want without asking?

Elias cannot hide behind the word "protect." He says what he wants. They agree that immediate danger may require action, but afterward he explains, listens and makes the next choice with her. Neither claims the future is settled.

The path divides. Elias points toward the easier river route. Zoe looks at the ridge and the open view.

ZOE
I want to go up there.

ELIAS
Then the ridge.

They take it together.
